%I A214032 #27 Sep 21 2026 22:41:57
%S A214032 17,67,109,137,181,191,229,233,239,257,283,307,311,349,353,359,479,
%T A214032 523,547,593,599,617,643,709,719,829,839,857,883,907,911,953,977,1021,
%U A214032 1031,1069,1097,1123,1151,1193,1217,1319,1433,1439,1483
%N A214032 Indices k where A214030(k) = k-2.
%C A214032 This sequence is to A214030 as A000057 is to A001177. It would be nice to have an interpretation of this sequence akin to the interpretation of A000057 as the set of primes which divide all Fibonacci sequences, having arbitrary initial values for a(1),a(2). The linearly recursive sequence which seems to be associated to this is 3*f(n)=6*f(n-1)+2*f(n-2), but this does not have integral values.
%C A214032 If we use the sequence 3,2,3,2,3,2.. instead of 2,3,2,3,... we end up with the same sequence a(n).
%C A214032 From _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Sep 15 2026: (Start)
%C A214032 With U, z_U and B as defined in A214031, the condition A214030(k) = k-2 corresponds to z_U(k) = k-1, which also forces k to be prime. Every such prime p satisfies (60/p) = 1.
%C A214032 For f(0) = a and f(1) = b, not both divisible by p, the corresponding solution has no term divisible by p if and only if 2*b^2 - 6*a*b - 3*a^2 == 0 (mod p). The two initial directions satisfying this congruence are the eigenlines of B. For each of the remaining p-1 initial directions, the corresponding solution contains exactly one multiple of p in every block of p-1 consecutive terms. This gives the analog of A214031, with two exceptional directions.
%C A214032 With its phase recorded, the projective action consists of two cycles of length p-1, each containing one infinity state, and two cycles of length 2 containing neither infinity state. The latter correspond to the exceptional directions. See A214031 for the 2,3 to 3,2 period reversal and the interpretation of DuPre's proposed characteristic equation.
%C A214032 (End)
%F A214032 {k: A214030(k) = k-2} = {k >= 2: z_U(k) = ord_k(-B) = k-1}, where ord_k denotes matrix order modulo n, with U and B as defined in A214031. - _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Sep 15 2026
%e A214032 Modulo 17, (-B)^8 == -I and thus (-B)^16 == I, so ord_17(-B) = 16 and A214030(17) = 15. The exceptional initial values satisfy b == 5*a or b == 15*a (mod 17). For example, the solution with f(0) = 1, f(1) = 5 satisfies f(2*j) == 11^j and f(2*j+1) == 5*11^j (mod 17), so none of its terms is divisible by 17. If b is congruent to neither 5*a nor 15*a modulo 17, every 16 consecutive terms contain exactly one multiple of 17. - _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Sep 15 2026
%o A214032 (PARI)
%o A214032 {b23(n)=local(t,m=1,s=[n]); if (n<2,0,while(1,
%o A214032 if(m%2,s=concat(s,2),s=concat(s,3));
%o A214032 t=contfracpnqn(concat(s,n));
%o A214032 t=contfrac(n*t[1,1]/t[2,1]);
%o A214032 if(t[1]<n^2||t[#t]<n^2,m++,break));m)};
%o A214032 /* To print the sequence A214032(n) to the screen, */
%o A214032 for(i=1,1500,if(b23(i)==i-2,print1(i,", ")));
%o A214032 (PARI)
%o A214032 {is32(n)= if(n<3 || n%2==0, return(0)); my(u=1, v=0, h=(n-1)/2); for(k=1, h, v=(2*u+v)%n; if(!v, return(k==h)); u=(3*v+u)%n; if(!u, return(0)); ); 0; };
%o A214032 for(n=1, 1500, if(is32(n), print1(n, ", "))); \\ _M. Jeremie Lafitte (Levitas)_, Sep 15 2026
%Y A214032 Cf. A214030, A214031, A000057, A001177.
%K A214032 nonn,changed
%O A214032 1,1
